A set is any collection of elements. Ways of defining a set:
- Enumeration: listing all elements, e.g., A = {1, 3, 5, 6, 9}
- Description by common properties: e.g., B = {x | x is an odd integer and x < 10}
The notation B = {x | P(x)} is read 'B is the set of all x such that P(x) holds.'

Types of sets:
- Finite set: has a finite number of elements, e.g., A = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10}
- Infinite set: has infinitely many elements, e.g., B = {x | x is an even integer}
- Null (Empty) set: has no elements, denoted ∅

Set operations:
- Union: A ∪ B = elements in A or B or both
- Intersection: A ∩ B = elements in both A and B; if A ∩ B = ∅, the sets are disjoint
- Difference: A – B = elements in A that are not in B (equivalently, A ∩ B')
- Complement: A' = U – A (all elements in the universal set U not in A)

For finite sets: |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| – |A ∩ B|. If |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B|, then A and B are disjoint (A ∩ B = ∅).

Example: A = {1,3,5,6,9}, B = {x | x is an odd integer}
- A ∪ B = {x | x = 6 or x is odd}
- A ∩ B = {1,3,5,9}
- A – B = {6}
- B' = {x | x is an even integer}
